<header><p class="st-kicker">county · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Meath</h1><p class="st-meta">1172 CE – 1241 CE · capital Trim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The great Anglo-Norman liberty of Meath granted to Hugh de Lacy, centred on the castle of Trim.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1172 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-ii-of-england">Henry II</a> granted the former <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mide">Kingdom of Mide</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath">Hugh de Lacy</a> to balance <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-de-clare-2nd-earl-of-pembroke">Strongbow's</a> power in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-leinster">Leinster</a>. De Lacy built motte castles across the territory and began the great stone castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trim-county-meath">Trim</a>, which became the largest Norman castle in Ireland.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mide">Meath</a> was a liberty with its own courts and officials. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-de-lacy-lord-of-meath">De Lacy</a> settled English tenants, founded towns and abbeys and married a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruaidri-ua-conchobair">Ruaidri Ua Conchobair</a>, the high king. He was killed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durrow-abbey">Durrow</a> in 1186. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-de-lacy-lord-of-meath">Walter de Lacy</a> recovered the lordship after a conflict with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> and held it until 1241.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Walter's death in 1241 the lordship was divided between his granddaughters, creating the liberties of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trim-county-meath">Trim</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-mide">Meath</a>. Meath later formed the heart of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-pale">English Pale</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 1172 CE – 1241 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">British Isles</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.02%;width:1.37%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1172 CE – 1241 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Trim</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Hugh de Lacy, Walter de Lacy</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Anglo-Norman French, Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Liberty of Meath</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1206&amp;focus=lordship-of-meath" aria-label="Lordship of Meath on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4529 337 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Meath lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4849 593L4853 592M4849 593L4861 599"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4853 592h0M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4849 593h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4849 593h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Trim. <a href="/atlas?year=1206&amp;focus=lordship-of-meath">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
